<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moesia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Moesia was a Roman province south of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, covering parts of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, named after the Thracian Moesi. Conquered after the campaigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Marcus Licinius Crassus</a> in 29 BCE and organised early in the 1st century CE, it was divided in 86 into Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia-inferior">Lower Moesia</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortresses guarded the Danube frontier, and from the 7th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> settled the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moesia.jpg" alt="Moesia" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:REmpire-Moesia.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
